@* Markup only — all logic lives in WebGlCanvas.razor.cs. No width/height attributes on purpose:
   webgl-canvas.js owns the backing buffer from the first measurement (see the ownership contract
   in the code-behind's class doc), and nothing draws before OnReady fires, so there is no
   flash-of-wrong-size content. touch-action/user-select are inline so consumers don't need a
   scoped-CSS bundle; display sizing is the consumer's job (via Class / an id-based CSS rule).

   POINTER events, not the mouse compatibility ones: webgl-canvas.js calls setPointerCapture on
   pointerdown, and capture is defined over the pointer family -- pairing it with @onmouse* left the
   capture governing one stream while the app listened to another, so a drag that left the canvas
   was delivered inconsistently across browsers. Touch is FILTERED OUT here (see the code-behind):
   the JS touch bridge already synthesises one-finger pan and two-finger pinch, and pointer events
   fire for fingers as well, so forwarding both would deliver every touch drag twice. *@
<canvas @ref="_canvasRef"
        id="@Id"
        tabindex="@TabIndex"
        class="@Class"
        style="touch-action:none;user-select:none;-webkit-user-select:none;"
        @onpointerdown="HandlePointerDownAsync"
        @onpointermove="HandlePointerMoveAsync"
        @onpointerup="HandlePointerUpAsync"
        @onpointercancel="HandlePointerCancelAsync"
        @onwheel="HandleWheelAsync"
        @onkeydown="HandleKeyDownAsync">
</canvas>
